{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite","id":1147785,"title":"Godly Kryptonite?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-10-07T08:59:36-07:00","id":1147785,"title":"Godly Kryptonite?","content":"So as most of us are probably aware by now, 1E Scion featured a pretty substantial power creep as you made your way up the ranks. I don't actually mind all that much, since the plot of the game is to become a literal God and challenge the living embodiments of reality itself to a show down.<br /> <br /> But one downside is that minions and the like became cannon fodder towards the end of the game. Investing points in combat followers was pretty much a waste, since they get killed off within the first few rounds of combat.<br /> <br /> <br /> But then I started thinking, and I realized that although they held the position of 'God,' these pagan deities weren't necessarily omnipotent like the monotheistic religions of today. In other words, they were reported to have some vulnerabilities that others could take advantage of.<br /> <br /> <br /> During the run of the old Hercules series, they used something called <a href=\"http://hercules-xena.wikia.com/wiki/Hind%27s_Blood\" target=\"_blank\">Hind's Blood</a> which served as a deadly poison to the otherwise invulnerable Gods of Olympus. However, I don't know if this was actually a part of Greek mythology, or just something they made up in the show.<br /> <br /> I understand that the Egyptian and Norse pantheons were still susceptible to the effects of time, although at a far slower rate than mortals. And they were apparently susceptible to violence, as well, since their mythology specifically mentions gods being killed and mutilated.<br /> <br /> <br /> If my interpretations are correct, then the Tuatha are the fairy lords of Ireland, and therefor might be susceptible to iron if you can't get them to break their geas. But I'd need more confirmation from somebody who knows more about Irish folk lore.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>So to reiterate my question: What are some weaknesses that the so called 'cannon fodder' might use to challenge the end game level players and actually have a chance at victory?</b>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T09:06:19-07:00","id":1147789,"title":"","content":"Maybe not direct kryptonite, but it probably wouldn't be too hard to learn who a now-deified Scion's mortal friends and family are, especially if their rise to godhood has been visible in the media. That might be a way to at least twist the Scion's arm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12628-yukikaze","name":"yukikaze","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12628_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T09:46:53-07:00","id":1147800,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>yukikaze</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147789#post1147789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe not direct kryptonite, but it probably wouldn't be too hard to learn who a now-deified Scion's mortal friends and family are, especially if their rise to godhood has been visible in the media. That might be a way to at least twist the Scion's arm.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Not really. Remember, this is a God they're threatening, and the standard tactics don't apply to them. Friends and family could be very easily protected by offering them a place of residence within your sanctum. How exactly are you going to kidnap somebody who lives on the moon, or a different solar system, or even another plane of reality? And in the event that you did kill a hostage, you'd be sending their soul to the afterlife, where the Gods are waiting for them. A quick interrogation and the God has all the details they need about the culprit, where they would then deliver righteous vengeance upon them.<br /> <br /> <br /> That's why I'm asking for things that would threaten the Gods on a more personal level. What could directly challenge their superiority over the mortal World?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T10:11:26-07:00","id":1147804,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite#post1147785\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>So to reiterate my question: What are some weaknesses that the so called 'cannon fodder' might use to challenge the end game level players and actually have a chance at victory?</b></div> </div> </div> </div>Good question! Just as you suspect Nyrufa, it depends Pantheon to Pantheon. For most Pantheons sadly the case will be, &quot;No Dice,&quot; the Gods are just better than you, than me, than mortals. There are some work-around ideas though.<br /> <br /> For the <b>Theoi</b>, sadly, no dice. The Hind's Blood isn't as far as I know mythological cannon for the Theoi, and actually runs counter to the main premise of Grecian Godhood. The Gods are all perfectly immortal, they can't die. There's a reason they're always locking each other away within the depths of the world, they're literally incapable of killing each other. The Gods, Titans, Hundred Handed Ones, Primordials, all of them are perfect immortals. They will never, ever die. Even when The Son of Zeus Prophecy is fulfilled, Zeus will not be killed, he will be overthrown. The 'point' of the Theoi is they are better than humans, so far above them that humans are but gnats to them, so providing a source to weaken, or otherwise make the Theoi not these perfectly immortal, unaging, undying beings sort of makes them not Theoi.<br /> <br /> There are, of course, ways around this. There is one instance that comes to mind of an immortal being incapacitated to the point of more-or-less dead. When Charon is poisoned by one of Heracles' arrows envenomed by the Lernean Hydra. The passage in question can be read here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> [Kheiron (Chiron)], you, immortal now and destined by your birthright to live on through all eternity, will long to die when you are tortured by the serpent's blood [i.e. poisoned by an arrow coated with Hydra's blood], that agonizing poison in your wounds; and, saved from immortality, the gods shall put you in death's power, and the three Goddesses [Moirai, Fates] shall unloose your threads of fate. </div> </div> </div>So, while the Theoi themselves are perfect, undying beings, the immortality they grant to others (potentially any Scion who undergoes Apotheosis, anyone who isn't 'pure Theoi') can be reversed in the case of emergencies such as this. So, Adonis, Heracles, Endymion, Ganymede, and Asclepius are all vulnerable to this.<br /> <br /> For the <b>Tuatha</b>, it's actually way easier than making them break their Geasa and using Iron. Iron probably wouldn't be any more or less helpful to the Tuatha themselves, they use Iron weapons, the Cold Iron thing is more of a folkloric thing to the Sidhe themselves, not necessarily the Tuatha. Getting them to break their Geasa would be a method of killing them, but you can also just stab them a bunch. Almost all of the major figures of the Pantheon are dead, killed by mortals during the invasion by the Sons of Mil. For whatever reason, either the mortals or Ireland are very good at killing Gods, or the Tuatha are just very frail for Deities. Other than this they are perfect, ageless beings often imagined as being untouched by original sin by Christian authors, so aging doesn't bother them.<br /> <br /> So, yeah. Just stab them a bunch. Shoot them, poison them. The Tuatha die easy, but their friends will come and kill everyone you have ever known, loved, seen, steal all of your stuff, destroy your home, and then kill you in a very brutal, embarrassing, and public way.<br /> <br /> The <b>Aesir</b> are trickier since several of them are Fated to die elsewhen, so you won't have much luck killing them until Ragnarok. Unless of course they die, and get brought back to life before then somehow. As you note, they are vulnerable to having the apples of Idun taken away from them though. Those whose Fates are unknown, however, you could totally kill. Far harder to kill than the Tuatha though, mortals don't go around killing Gods for the Aesir, it's mostly God-on-God violence for them.<br /> <br /> So, while they are not quite as beyond the reach of mortals as the Theoi, I don't think the 'Cannon Fodder' will ever have much of a hope against the Aesir unless Fate has decided one of them's going to die by mortal hands.<br /> <br /> The <b>Teotl</b> are somewhat trickier since while they can be maimed, and they can die, it isn't the purview of mortals to do so. Also mortals probably wouldn't <i>want</i> to do so. Without the Teotl, the Fifth Sun is ending, and all of humanity is dying off in the blink of an eye. The Teotl are also rather good at not dying, or dying and coming back, so even if a mortal somehow managed to do the impossible (and the really stupid) and kill one of the Teotl, they could, and probably would bounce back very quickly thereafter.<br /> <br /> So, again. While not totally out of reach like the Theoi, 'Cannon Fodder' isn't going to have much of a hope in hell against the Teotl. Even if you try to starve them for power and cut off all blood sacrifice to their name, all that means is you are going to end up ending the Fifth Sun. The Teotl will be fine, but they just won't be able to defend the Fifth Sun and keep the world working. They will start again on The Sixth Sun, and maybe try giants again instead of humans.<br /> <br /> If your main concern is Followers and Creatures being weak at the Demigod and God tier, it might be easier to look for houserules to make the Birthright more balanced at those tiers. Sadly, for mortals, we can never hope to aspire to the greatness of the Divine, not but dust before ageless eyes. Except for the Tuatha who are very mortal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 10-07-2017, 01:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T10:11:44-07:00","id":1147805,"title":"","content":"One possibility with the Hindu Gods is to borrow an Astra or gain a Boon from another God (or even the same one). Brahma has given out multiple Boons to people who've used them to kick Indra and his entire court out of Svarga, and Shiva at one point gave out a Boon to someone to be able to blow up anyone's head by touching it... which he promptly tried to use on Shiva himself. Vishnu had to save him from that mess by seducing the guy into touching his own head. Many of the wielder's of Astras and Boons are Asura titanspawn or Scion Heroes, but there's technically nothing stopping a mortal from impressing a God enough to acquire one.<br /> <br /> The other way would be to achieve Divine Enlightenment and become a Rishi... which any old mortal can <i>technically</i> do... Rishis have performed such varied feats as turning the Trimurti into babies in order to breast feed them, forced Sarasvati to become human for daring to correct their grammar, created a second Svarga and carbon copies of all the Gods because they didn't like the current ones, petrified Indra and his entire court and kept them around as lawn ornaments, and casually blown 60,000 people to smithereens for calling them a horse thief.<br /> <br /> The thing to remember about most Hindu Gods is that they are Duty bound to honor not just their own but their compatriot's promises as well... impress one of them enough to get them to agree to something stupid, and the entire Pantheon then has to back them up... which is why Vishnu and Durga spend half of mythology figuring out ways to kill the Boon given individuals without breaking any of the tenets of the Boons they've been given, irrespective of the fact that normally either of them could kill every single one of them ten ways to Tuesday without breaking a sweat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T10:21:50-07:00","id":1147809,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147804#post1147804\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Good question! Just as you suspect Nyrufa, it depends Pantheon to Pantheon. For most Pantheons sadly the case will be, &quot;No Dice,&quot; the Gods are just better than you, than me, than mortals. There are some work-around ideas though.<br /> <br /> For the <b>Theoi</b>, sadly, no dice. The Hind's Blood isn't as far as I know mythological cannon for the Theoi, and actually runs counter to the main premise of Grecian Godhood. The Gods are all perfectly immortal, they can't die. There's a reason they're always locking each other away within the depths of the world, they're literally incapable of killing each other. The Gods, Titans, Hundred Handed Ones, Primordials, all of them are perfect immortals. They will never, ever die. Even when The Son of Zeus Prophecy is fulfilled, Zeus will not be killed, he will be overthrown. The 'point' of the Theoi is they are better than humans, so far above them that humans are but gnats to them, so providing a source to weaken, or otherwise make the Theoi not these perfectly immortal, unaging, undying beings sort of makes them not Theoi.<br /> <br /> There are, of course, ways around this. There is one instance that comes to mind of an immortal being incapacitated to the point of more-or-less dead. When Charon is poisoned by one of Heracles' arrows envenomed by the Lernean Hydra. The passage in question can be read here.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So, while the Theoi themselves are perfect, undying beings, the immortality they grant to others (potentially any Scion who undergoes Apotheosis, anyone who isn't 'pure Theoi') can be reversed in the case of emergencies such as this. So, Adonis, Heracles, Endymion, Ganymede, and Asclepius are all vulnerable to this.<br /> <br /> For the <b>Tuatha</b>, it's actually way easier than making them break their Geasa and using Iron. Iron probably wouldn't be any more or less helpful to the Tuatha themselves, they use Iron weapons, the Cold Iron thing is more of a folkloric thing to the Sidhe themselves, not necessarily the Tuatha. Getting them to break their Geasa would be a method of killing them, but you can also just stab them a bunch. Almost all of the major figures of the Pantheon are dead, killed by mortals during the invasion by the Sons of Mil. For whatever reason, either the mortals or Ireland are very good at killing Gods, or the Tuatha are just very frail for Deities. Other than this they are perfect, ageless beings often imagined as being untouched by original sin by Christian authors, so aging doesn't bother them.<br /> <br /> So, yeah. Just stab them a bunch. Shoot them, poison them. The Tuatha die easy, but their friends will come and kill everyone you have ever known, loved, seen, steal all of your stuff, destroy your home, and then kill you in a very brutal, embarrassing, and public way.<br /> <br /> The <b>Aesir</b> are trickier since several of them are Fated to die elsewhen, so you won't have much luck killing them until Ragnarok. Unless of course they die, and get brought back to life before then somehow. As you note, they are vulnerable to having the apples of Idun taken away from them though. Those whose Fates are unknown, however, you could totally kill. Far harder to kill than the Tuatha though, mortals don't go around killing Gods for the Aesir, it's mostly God-on-God violence for them.<br /> <br /> So, while they are not quite as beyond the reach of mortals as the Theoi, I don't think the 'Cannon Fodder' will ever have much of a hope against the Aesir unless Fate has decided one of them's going to die by mortal hands.<br /> <br /> The <b>Teotl</b> are somewhat trickier since while they can be maimed, and they can die, it isn't the purview of mortals to do so. Also mortals probably wouldn't <i>want</i> to do so. Without the Teotl, the Fifth Sun is ending, and all of humanity is dying off in the blink of an eye. The Teotl are also rather good at not dying, or dying and coming back, so even if a mortal somehow managed to do the impossible (and the really stupid) and kill one of the Teotl, they could, and probably would bounce back very quickly thereafter.<br /> <br /> So, again. While not totally out of reach like the Theoi, 'Cannon Fodder' isn't going to have much of a hope in hell against the Teotl. Even if you try to starve them for power and cut off all blood sacrifice to their name, all that means is you are going to end up ending the Fifth Sun. The Teotl will be fine, but they just won't be able to defend the Fifth Sun and keep the world working. They will start again on The Sixth Sun, and maybe try giants again instead of humans.<br /> <br /> If your main concern is Followers and Creatures being weak at the Demigod and God tier, it might be easier to look for houserules to make the Birthright more balanced at those tiers. Sadly, for mortals, we can never hope to aspire to the greatness of the Divine, not but dust before ageless eyes. Except for the Tuatha who are very mortal.</div> </div> </div> </div>Correct me if I'm wrong,but wasn't there two powerful gigantes that managed to weaken Ares until he was super powerless and stuck him in a pot?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T10:27:54-07:00","id":1147810,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147809#post1147809\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Correct me if I'm wrong,but wasn't there two powerful gigantes that managed to weaken Ares until he was super powerless and stuck him in a pot?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yup! That's the imprisonment motif we see with the Theoi. No one can kill anyone else, but they can sure as hell put each other in 'boxes' of various sorts. Ares gets put in a pot, Zeus has all of his tendons torn out, Atlas has to hold up the sky, Prometheus is chained to the mountain, Typhon is trapped under a mountain (also sometimes in Tartarus), the majority of the Titans are in Tartarus, the Hundred Handed Ones and the Primordial Cyclopses were locked in Tartarus by Cronus, even the castration of Ouranos pretty much puts him in a 'box' making him no longer a threat. Thanatos gets trapped once too, by a mortal actually. Goes badly for him in the end.<br /> <br /> The Theoi can't kill each other, but they can be trapped, imprisoned, or rendered temporarily inert. It's a fun little theme! I'm always tempted to have God-tier Theoi have their health work differently from everyone else. Instead of dying, you just wind up imprisoned, rendered inert, or trapped somehow.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T10:32:18-07:00","id":1147811,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147810#post1147810\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Theoi can't kill each other, but they can be trapped, imprisoned, or rendered temporarily inert. It's a fun little theme! I'm always tempted to have God-tier Theoi have their health work differently from everyone else. Instead of dying, you just wind up imprisoned, rendered inert, or trapped somehow.</div> </div> </div> </div>That actually sounds like it could be an interesting God level Metamorphosis Boon <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Can't speak for what kind of mechanical nightmare it would be to balance though <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T10:41:54-07:00","id":1147817,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147810#post1147810\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Thanatos gets trapped once too, by a mortal actually. Goes badly for him in the end.<br /> <br /> The Theoi can't kill each other, but they can be trapped, imprisoned, or rendered temporarily inert. It's a fun little theme! I'm always tempted to have God-tier Theoi have their health work differently from everyone else. Instead of dying, you just wind up imprisoned, rendered inert, or trapped somehow.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Isn't Thanatos the god of death itself? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Also, I like that idea of having their health work differently. Maybe when they run out, they just sort of fall unconscious while their bodies try to heal themselves.<br /> <br /> But, even if they can't outright be killed, the fact they could be crippled or sealed away means that mortals would still have a fighting chance against them. Sure, you'd have to keep them under maximum security, because they'd be gunning for your ass in the event they escaped. But 'immortal' doesn't mean 'infallible.'<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147811#post1147811\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That actually sounds like it could be an interesting God level Metamorphosis Boon <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Can't speak for what kind of mechanical nightmare it would be to balance though <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Wouldn't be too unbalanced, considering the enemy would have the same protection. Fights would turn into an endurance test of seeing who could take more punishment before collapsing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 10-07-2017, 01:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T10:53:18-07:00","id":1147824,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147817#post1147817\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Isn't Thanatos the god of death itself? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>He is their Death God! Specifically their, like, 'naturally occurring death' God... or 'Spirit' sort of because the Theoi really <i>love</i> being granularly specific about everything they do.<br /> <br /> Theoretically a mortal could try to imprison one of the Theoi in that same way, but it might go badly for everyone, similarly to the problem we see with the Teotl. When Thanatos is locked away, things stop dying of natural causes IIRC, which throws everything out of whack and the Theoi have to look into what's causing their world to have gone funny. Lock up Apollo, and you've got eternal night, that sort of problem.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147817#post1147817\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, I like that idea of having their health work differently. Maybe when they run out, they just sort of fall unconscious while their bodies try to heal themselves.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147811#post1147811\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That actually sounds like it could be an interesting God level Metamorphosis Boon <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Can't speak for what kind of mechanical nightmare it would be to balance though <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>I am glad you both like the idea! I have always wanted to differentiate the Pantheons from each other more than just their PsPs, looking more at the different ideas of just what being a 'God' means to each culture in question. It is a <i>huge pain</i> to do in 1e, I tried, lots of wasted pencil lead on that. In 1e the best mechanical 'method' for it is making them sort-of-Relics, but it's a really clunky way to go about it that I didn't really like. With luck there 2e will provide a better method for pulling it off.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 10-07-2017, 01:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T11:01:06-07:00","id":1147827,"title":"","content":"There is an obscure little creature from Greek myth referenced by only a single source called the ophiotaurus. Part serpent and part bull, whoever burnt the entrails of this creature would be granted the power to defeat the Gods. <br /> <br /> Now...exactly what that means we don\u2019t know. According to the myth, an ally of the Titans did manage to slay the ophiotaurus, but Zeus sent an eagle to snatch the entrails before they could be burnt. <br /> <br /> Which means that if you want to include such a creature in your games, you have some freedom to determine what happens if the ritual is completed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9296-zeldafan42","name":"Zeldafan42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9296_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T11:11:39-07:00","id":1147828,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147824#post1147824\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am glad you both like the idea! I have always wanted to differentiate the Pantheons from each other more than just their PsPs, looking more at the different ideas of just what being a 'God' means to each culture in question. It is a <i>huge pain</i> to do in 1e, I tried, lots of wasted pencil lead on that. In 1e the best mechanical 'method' for it is making them sort-of-Relics, but it's a really clunky way to go about it that I didn't really like. With luck there 2e will provide a better method for pulling it off.</div> </div> </div> </div>IIRC Neall once mentioned that the 2e PSPs are supposed to be wide enough to include all the quirks various Pantheons display... hence why so many of them are less 'power sets' and more 'power sources' (thematically speaking of course, obviously mechanically they all have powers). So, Egyptians have Heku, the sorcery of the soul, and isn't all power in and magic and miracle in Egyptian mythology fundamentally derived from the different parts of the soul and their sorcery? Similarly for the Deva, their PSP is Yoga, and all power in Hindu myth is derived from Yoga... it is the source of all miracles, and therefore you could fluff any random thing the Hindu Gods do that others don't as a Yoga boon.<br /> <br /> For the Greeks the two things I'd love to see at God Level are firstly the whole absolute immortality thing, and secondly the whole 'you cannot grasp the true true form' thing they have going. IIRC, the Greek Gods never appear to mortals in their true forms because they'll burn to cinders. They always appear to mortals shape-shifted, even when they're not even remotely trying to hide their divinity (like the passage in the Aeneid I believe where Apollo appears to the Trojans as one of their comrades but is also floating in the air and making Godly pronouncements so no one is remotely that this is a God).<br /> <br /> I admit Absolute Immortality might be a bit of a stretch for Metamorphosis, but the other thing, the idea of having a 'true form' that you can reveal to kill off any mortals in the area, sounds like an absolutely perfect God level Metamorphosis power.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T11:26:19-07:00","id":1147832,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147828#post1147828\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> For the Greeks the two things I'd love to see at God Level are firstly the whole absolute immortality thing, and secondly the whole 'you cannot grasp the true true form' thing they have going. IIRC, the Greek Gods never appear to mortals in their true forms because they'll burn to cinders. They always appear to mortals shape-shifted, even when they're not even remotely trying to hide their divinity (like the passage in the Aeneid I believe where Apollo appears to the Trojans as one of their comrades but is also floating in the air and making Godly pronouncements so no one is remotely that this is a God).<br /> <br /> I admit Absolute Immortality might be a bit of a stretch for Metamorphosis, but the other thing, the idea of having a 'true form' that you can reveal to kill off any mortals in the area, sounds like an absolutely perfect God level Metamorphosis power.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Typhon was described as being comprised of hundreds of living animals and was so massive that his head reached the stars and his arms spanned across the horizon. In other words, he's a chimera the size of a planet! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T11:40:58-07:00","id":1147834,"title":"","content":"I am under the impression that without epic stamina,strength or dexterity or warrior knacks a god is vulnerable at battle. So if you shoot a god without this particular powerset,they can be hurt. Even if it's just a normal gun. I also got the impression that a god's power level is very flexible,Neall said that to create a powerful incarnation a god has to attach omens to the incarnation,that gave me the impression that the gods sometimes visit the World in weak incarnations,when they want just to eat McDonald's without worrying about their omens. Then it might be easier for a weaker being to get them","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4621,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","user":"Nicolas Milioni","date":". 10-07-2017, 02:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-07T11:45:29-07:00","id":1147837,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147834#post1147834\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am under the impression that without epic stamina,strength or dexterity or warrior knacks a god is vulnerable at battle. So if you shoot a god without this particular powerset,they can be hurt. Even if it's just a normal gun.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Well, technically, yes. But even without epic attributes, they can still raise their normal attributes far higher than any mortal can. Ordinary mortals cap out at 5, while Legendary beings can go upwards of like 15, or something like that.<br /> <br /> That's roughly 3x as many attribute levels as your typical mortal who's achieved record holding status.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T05:14:35-07:00","id":1147975,"title":"","content":"my godly kryptonite is:<br /> <br /> Gold made by the soul of humans<br /> <br /> <br /> The reason is: gods exist by the man, so a stone made with the soul of a man would hurts the gods","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12508-elanconnor","name":"ElanConnor","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12508_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"172"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T06:55:47-07:00","id":1147992,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1147804#post1147804\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> For the <b>Theoi</b>, sadly, no dice. The Hind's Blood isn't as far as I know mythological cannon for the Theoi, and actually runs counter to the main premise of Grecian Godhood. The Gods are all perfectly immortal, they can't die. There's a reason they're always locking each other away within the depths of the world, they're literally incapable of killing each other. The Gods, Titans, Hundred Handed Ones, Primordials, all of them are perfect immortals. They will never, ever die. Even when The Son of Zeus Prophecy is fulfilled, Zeus will not be killed, he will be overthrown. The 'point' of the Theoi is they are better than humans, so far above them that humans are but gnats to them, so providing a source to weaken, or otherwise make the Theoi not these perfectly immortal, unaging, undying beings sort of makes them not Theoi.<br /> <br /> There are, of course, ways around this. There is one instance that comes to mind of an immortal being incapacitated to the point of more-or-less dead. When Charon is poisoned by one of Heracles' arrows envenomed by the Lernean Hydra.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> So, basically, the Theoi are Kars?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xAXIbYI9RgU\" data-code=\"xAXIbYI9RgU\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/xAXIbYI9RgU/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 10-08-2017, 10:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T17:46:02-07:00","id":1148177,"title":"","content":"Is being irreparably dismembered and/or imprisoned in the lowest depths of the underworld (you know... where the dead go) substantive different than death? I mean, other than being an even worse experience for the god/Titan in question?<br /> <br /> Also keep in mind the immortal nature of Mantles provides cover in Scion for immortals being killable but still considered immortal. Neall\u2019s mentioned that may be why Aphrodite has two distinct birth myths.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T18:04:30-07:00","id":1148182,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148177#post1148177\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is being irreparably dismembered and/or imprisoned in the lowest depths of the underworld (you know... where the dead go) substantive different than death? I mean, other than being an even worse experience for the god/Titan in question?</div> </div> </div> </div>This actually prompted me to check something out which is kinda interesting! Tartarus and the Grecian Underworld has been used as a prison longer than it was used as an afterlife interestingly! The first mortals, the mortals from the Golden Age of Cronus were ageless, deathless, suffered no sickness, and generally lived perfect lovely lives free from 'bad stuff.' Tartarus and underground had been used as a prison since the time of Ouranos where the Titans, Hundred Handed Ones, and the Three Cyclopses were imprisoned. Hades isn't used as an afterlife until after the reign of Zeus begins. So it's less like the Titans are imprisoned where the dead go, but the dead are kept in the front garden of a traditional super-prison.<br /> <br /> But, on an actual functional level, I don't think being trapped in the underworld is enormously different from being dead, it's essentially like being dead. However, importantly, the Titans don't have their memory wiped, and don't undergo the 'Fading' that the majority of mortal shades do. I suppose it also means they are able to just 'leave' if they can manage to get past the Hundred Handed Ones guarding the door. Where Hades is able to keep the dead in, I expect he alone might struggle to deal with the Titans if they managed to get through the bronze gates.<br /> <br /> The idea of the Theoi, Titans, and their ilk all being immortal is pretty important for Greek cosmology. &quot;The Deathless Gods,&quot; and &quot;The Deathless Ones,&quot; are terms used with <i>exceptional</i> frequency by the Greeks, they entirely believed their Deities to be beings who are beyond death. It's pretty neat, and I think pretty unique compared to a lot of Pantheons who tend to have their Deities entirely capable of dying given the right situations, mostly due to God-on-God violence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T18:16:59-07:00","id":1148185,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148182#post1148182\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This actually prompted me to check something out which is kinda interesting! Tartarus and the Grecian Underworld has been used as a prison longer than it was used as an afterlife interestingly! The first mortals, the mortals from the Golden Age of Cronus were ageless, deathless, suffered no sickness, and generally lived perfect lovely lives free from 'bad stuff.'</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> If that's the case, then why the hell aren't mortals siding with the Titans against the Theoi?<br /> <br /> The Greek Underworld is pretty frickin depressing. Only the best of the best get to go to any thing remotely similar to heaven (Elysian Fields) and you need to do that 3 consecutive times before you're allowed to retire on the <i>Isles of the Blessed</i>.<br /> <br /> I used to be a huge fan of Greek mythology. But the more I learn about them, the more I realize they're a bunch of giant dicks! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> The only one who was even remotely worthy of respect was Hades, and the only dickish thing he ever did was against a fellow deity. I mean, unless the mortal intentionally picked a fight with him...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 10-08-2017, 09:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T18:44:18-07:00","id":1148193,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148185#post1148185\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> If that's the case, then why the hell aren't mortals siding with the Titans against the Theoi?</div> </div> </div> </div>Easy enough to imagine Prometheus\u2019 original race of gold were a tiny race who lived idyllically but couldn\u2019t expand or develop (which we know Prometheus wanted for humanity). Hesiod\u2019s \u201cIron race\u201d (modern mortals) would never have existed under Kronus in the first place, suffering or not.<br /> <br /> As for how depressing the Greek afterlife is - it\u2019s literally no different in that regard from any other ancient European or West Asian afterlife setup. Anyone saying otherwise is selling something.<br /> <br /> I mean, the Aesir are often sited as more benevolent toward humanity than the Olympians, but unless you literally die in combat sword-in-Hand, you\u2019re going to rot in Helheim. And even Valhalla only exists to provide Odin warriors, not out of any benevolence.<br /> <br /> Gods being dicks is pretty universal among ancient pantheons. It\u2019s not the case with (for example) modern Hindu deities because said religion kept up with moral evolution of civilization, instead of us just looking at an ancient snapshot of cultural values as with a long dead belief system.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 10-09-2017, 07:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T18:47:42-07:00","id":1148194,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148185#post1148185\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> If that's the case, then why the hell aren't mortals siding with the Titans against the Theoi?<br /> <br /> The Greek Underworld is pretty frickin depressing. Only the best of the best get to go to any thing remotely similar to heaven (Elysian Fields) and you need to do that 3 consecutive times before you're allowed to retire on the <i>Isles of the Blessed</i>.<br /> <br /> I used to be a huge fan of Greek mythology. But the more I learn about them, the more I realize they're a bunch of giant dicks! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> The only one who was even remotely worthy of respect was Hades, and the only dickish thing he ever did was against a fellow deity. I mean, unless the mortal intentionally picked a fight with him...</div> </div> </div> </div>Your average greek hero wasn't a dick though,they were often good people who wanted glory but not into the expense of others. Although the all of them had fatal flaws. Although with that being said,I'm happy scion will let us plays the monsters too,I want to apply the themes of good old Beast to scion","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T19:19:54-07:00","id":1148200,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148185#post1148185\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If that's the case, then why the hell aren't mortals siding with the Titans against the Theoi?<br /> <br /> The Greek Underworld is pretty frickin depressing. Only the best of the best get to go to any thing remotely similar to heaven (Elysian Fields) and you need to do that 3 consecutive times before you're allowed to retire on the <i>Isles of the Blessed</i>.<br /> <br /> I used to be a huge fan of Greek mythology. But the more I learn about them, the more I realize they're a bunch of giant dicks! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> The only one who was even remotely worthy of respect was Hades, and the only dickish thing he ever did was against a fellow deity. I mean, unless the mortal intentionally picked a fight with him...</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a good question! I can't speak for the Ancient Greeks, them being dead and all, but I can sort of try to riddle things out on the subject!<br /> <br /> So, the first thing, the topic of the Afterlife. Afterlives being nice is pretty uncommon, I think I've talked about it before, but the vast majority of them are really not fun places. The idea of paradises for humans to live in after death is a relatively uncommon notion, so the Greeks wouldn't think of it like, &quot;Man <i>our </i>afterlife sucks,&quot; but more as &quot;Life is pretty great, being dead sucks.&quot; They wouldn't blame the Gods for this necessarily, this is just the way it is. The Greek Underworld is pretty obviously influenced by the Mesopotamian Underworld, but comparatively, the Greek one is pretty awesome! For the Greeks, you have a chance for a nice afterlife. The Mesopotamians, not so much.<br /> <br /> To build on this, there were ways for people who were really concerned about the afterlife to express this concern in a religious way, though we don't have awesome records on this. You touch on one of these notions in your post, the Mystery Cults. Be they the Eleusinian, Orphic, or some of the other less well attested ones. The details are lost to us, but participating in these cults, one was able to secure a spot in the Lethean Fields for themselves. The Isle of the Blessed was originally reserved for the Capital-H Heroes, but as you mentioned, later traditions which start playing with ideas of reincarnation kick off an idea where dying after gaining entrance to the Lethean Fields three times would stop your reincarnation cycle and let you hang out on the Isle of the Blessed.<br /> <br /> However, these notions were not... unified, there was a lot of individual interpretation of these things, and individual understanding. Not all Greeks believed in the Orphic reincarnation system for example, some believed the Lethean Fields and the Isle of the Blessed were the same location. As it wasn't a centralized unified religious system, it's a bit all over the place.<br /> <br /> But, right, anyways. On the death front, the reason mortals wouldn't despise the Gods for it has three major reasons. Reason #1: That's the way it is, it's like getting upset with Gravity. Reason #2: The Gods <i>do</i> offer you a way around it by participating in Mystery Cults (which you may or may not believe in), they are willing to show (?) mercy (?) to mortals or perhaps the ritual activities of the mysteries make a mortal capable of reaching those locations. It's not super clear exactly how it 'worked' like in a theological sense. Reason #3: They didn't necessarily have a concept of a paradise afterlife as a common everyday notion as we do in the modern period due to the influence of the Abrahamic Faiths on our everyday life, you can't long for what you don't believe in.<br /> <br /> The second thing, the topic of the Theoi being dicks, is sort of more complicated. Think of it this way, myths are fundamentally stories, and stories need bad things to happen in order to be interesting. They need drama, excitement, and the like. The Gods, as the very embodiments of literally everything in existence, are often the catalyst for these stories. So, to us looking back, the Theoi appear to be <i>giant </i>dicks since our only window to them is their 'bad days' more or less. People in Ancient Greece experienced them differently. Every kindness that you were given when you were in need was because Zeus was the enforcer of such things, you knew people needed to follow the laws of Zenea (think like Hospitality) because Zeus was keeping an eye on that shit. Every time you recovered from an illness, hey, someone was looking out for you! When you gave birth and didn't die? It's all cool, you weren't Fated to die! Or maybe Hera or Artemis intervened. You woke up in the nick of time to notice one of your sheep was wondering off, Pan's got your back. You know when you die, you will be judged fairly by the judges of the Underworld. You know that spending all this time as part of the Eleusinian Mysteries means Demeter is making sure you have a good spot in the Underworld.<br /> <br /> In some of the plays, such as Antigone, we get to see the kind side of the Gods when it comes to death. Her brother Polynices is denied a proper burial, will be denied ritual mourning, and anyone who tries to preform any of these death rituals is going to be killed for it. Antigone ignores the mortal law, and follows the Divine Law, and is punished by mortal hands for it. She is entombed with her dead brother, where she commits suicide before the mortal authorities decide that this actually has gone way too far. The Gods don't take action, but their presence is felt through this idea of the Divine superseding the mortal. The Gods want the dead disposed of properly, they don't want bodies disrespected (which is why the scene between Achilles and the corpse of Hector is a big deal) and their will of this proper treatment of the dead, even if that dead person was a criminal, is more important than whatever your local king thinks. You know that the Gods have your back, that at the end of the day, you're not going to be the one catching the ire of the divine.<br /> <br /> Edit: I thought of another example of this. When a mortal breaks <i>Divine Law</i>, the Gods actually can sort it all out. When Agamemnon is murdered by his wife and her lover, his son, Orestes, kills his mother and her lover. This is totally in violation of Divine Law, no killing your parents, bad you. The Furies arrive on the scene, and then everything gets complicated. See, Apollo had ordered him to kill his mother for killing his father, so now everything just became a jurisdictional mess more or less. So, in the next play in the trilogy, we see this resolve through what's essentially a court case. Apollo and Athena set up a trial for Orestes is given with mortals voting as a jury. Apollo presents his case, the Furies present their case, the jury is tied, and Athena gives a tie-breaking vote to spare him. The furies are a bit miffed their authority just got overruled, but, the pair of them are favored children of Zeus. And then Athena essentially bribes them by giving them a new name, &quot;The Kindly Ones,&quot; essentially going, &quot;Oh you're so nice for sparing him, you are the Kindly Ones now, not the Furies,&quot; and they like the sound of that, so they go along with it. So, you can see from this the Theoi actually taking a hands on approach to things, and making sure stuff is fair. Or, well, fair for a Bronze Age society. Or any human society.<br /> <br /> The Theoi are literally everything, they aren't people, we make them look like people. They're more like abstract notions that we mush into the shapes of people to understand them better, potentially why shapeshifting is such a big part of Greek mythology. They make sure the laws work, they make sure people are born, and that people die. (since when people <i>stop</i> dying in the myth of Thanatos' capture, things go funky) They make sure your animals give birth, that your crops grow, that you catch fish, that you come home safely from war, that you become wealthy, that you got married to your childhood sweetheart. They <i>also</i> will slap you for being a shit, offend them, don't venerate them, you know they'll be annoyed and will probably start making your life hard. <i>However</i>, you know that this is pretty much a fair system. You know that if you're a shit, if the King is shit, if a slave is shit, you're all going to get the ire of the Theoi on you. That's a pretty powerful idea, and one that people tend to like, that <i>something</i> is fair, especially when we live in a fundamentally unfair world.<br /> <br /> And, lastly. Why wouldn't the Greeks side with the Titans. Well, as we have seen here, the Theoi are... not awesome, but more or less decent for the ancient Greeks. This is the way it's always been, it would be very similar to someone disliking Gravity, or other natural laws. Secondly, the Golden Age of Cronus is... a debated topic by the Greeks. They were never totally sure one way or another if it was a good thing. It was lawless, there were no cities, there was always food, but essentially there wasn't 'society' on a level we would really get. It was just people, wandering around in the nude, just... being. Zeus arriving, ending this, and giving humanity Law generally gets seen as a good move, people like laws, they're predictable, and they can 'get' them.<br /> <br /> Secondly, the Greeks didn't necessarily side <i>against</i> the Titans, it's just how the world was. You want some good crops? Pray to Demeter, not Cronus. It'd be like filing a complaint with someone who was retired and hadn't worked at the company for a decade, it's pointless, they can't do anything. And you're not about to get them hired back somehow. There would be no point to siding with the Titans, you can't do anything about it, and they (mostly, with exceptions to those not locked up of course) don't have any influence in the world. The interactions between Gods and Humans was because through the Gods, Humans could hope to influence their world around them. They couldn't do that with the imprisoned Titans.<br /> <br /> Thirdly, mortals probably didn't <i>want</i> the Titans breaking free the same reason I don't want people to start tossing Nukes around. These are beings who can hold up the sky, throw mountains, flood the planet, and they <i>fought</i> once. Here, where you live. And everyone <i>died</i>. So, maybe let's not have a gigantic war that kills us all and needs <i>another</i> generation of humans to be made up by whoever emerges the victor. The Age of Iron isn't great, but hey, at least it's not the Age of Lead. The Titans sure as hell aren't going to be nicer to you, they will also think you are annoying gnats. It would just be the same shit with a different set of names, with an apocalypse added in to kill you and everyone you love.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 10-08-2017, 10:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T19:43:46-07:00","id":1148204,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148200#post1148200\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's a good question! I can't speak for the Ancient Greeks, them being dead and all, but I can sort of try to riddle things out on the subject!<br /> <br /> So, the first thing, the topic of the Afterlife. Afterlives being nice is pretty uncommon, I think I've talked about it before, but the vast majority of them are really not fun places. The idea of paradises for humans to live in after death is a relatively uncommon notion, so the Greeks wouldn't think of it like, &quot;Man <i>our </i>afterlife sucks,&quot; but more as &quot;Life is pretty great, being dead sucks.&quot; They wouldn't blame the Gods for this necessarily, this is just the way it is. The Greek Underworld is pretty obviously influenced by the Mesopotamian Underworld, but comparatively, the Greek one is pretty awesome! For the Greeks, you have a chance for a nice afterlife. The Mesopotamians, not so much.<br /> <br /> To build on this, there were ways for people who were really concerned about the afterlife to express this concern in a religious way, though we don't have awesome records on this. You touch on one of these notions in your post, the Mystery Cults. Be they the Eleusinian, Orphic, or some of the other less well attested ones. The details are lost to us, but participating in these cults, one was able to secure a spot in the Lethean Fields for themselves. The Isle of the Blessed was originally reserved for the Capital-H Heroes, but as you mentioned, later traditions which start playing with ideas of reincarnation kick off an idea where dying after gaining entrance to the Lethean Fields three times would stop your reincarnation cycle and let you hang out on the Isle of the Blessed.<br /> <br /> However, these notions were not... unified, there was a lot of individual interpretation of these things, and individual understanding. Not all Greeks believed in the Orphic reincarnation system for example, some believed the Lethean Fields and the Isle of the Blessed were the same location. As it wasn't a centralized unified religious system, it's a bit all over the place.<br /> <br /> But, right, anyways. On the death front, the reason mortals wouldn't despise the Gods for it has three major reasons. Reason #1: That's the way it is, it's like getting upset with Gravity. Reason #2: The Gods <i>do</i> offer you a way around it by participating in Mystery Cults (which you may or may not believe in), they are willing to show (?) mercy (?) to mortals or perhaps the ritual activities of the mysteries make a mortal capable of reaching those locations. It's not super clear exactly how it 'worked' like in a theological sense. Reason #3: They didn't necessarily have a concept of a paradise afterlife as a common everyday notion as we do in the modern period due to the influence of the Abrahamic Faiths on our everyday life, you can't long for what you don't believe in.<br /> <br /> The second thing, the topic of the Theoi being dicks, is sort of more complicated. Think of it this way, myths are fundamentally stories, and stories need bad things to happen in order to be interesting. They need drama, excitement, and the like. The Gods, as the very embodiments of literally everything in existence, are often the catalyst for these stories. So, to us looking back, the Theoi appear to be <i>giant </i>dicks since our only window to them is their 'bad days' more or less. People in Ancient Greece experienced them differently. Every kindness that you were given when you were in need was because Zeus was the enforcer of such things, you knew people needed to follow the laws of Zenea (think like Hospitality) because Zeus was keeping an eye on that shit. Every time you recovered from an illness, hey, someone was looking out for you! When you gave birth and didn't die? It's all cool, you weren't Fated to die! Or maybe Hera or Artemis intervened. You woke up in the nick of time to notice one of your sheep was wondering off, Pan's got your back. You know when you die, you will be judged fairly by the judges of the Underworld. You know that spending all this time as part of the Eleusinian Mysteries means Demeter is making sure you have a good spot in the Underworld.<br /> <br /> In some of the plays, such as Antigone, we get to see the kind side of the Gods when it comes to death. Her brother Polynices is denied a proper burial, will be denied ritual mourning, and anyone who tries to preform any of these death rituals is going to be killed for it. Antigone ignores the mortal law, and follows the Divine Law, and is punished by mortal hands for it. She is entombed with her dead brother, where she commits suicide before the mortal authorities decide that this actually has gone way too far. The Gods don't take action, but their presence is felt through this idea of the Divine superseding the mortal. The Gods want the dead disposed of properly, they don't want bodies disrespected (which is why the scene between Achilles and the corpse of Hector is a big deal) and their will of this proper treatment of the dead, even if that dead person was a criminal, is more important than whatever your local king thinks. You know that the Gods have your back, that at the end of the day, you're not going to be the one catching the ire of the divine.<br /> <br /> Edit: I thought of another example of this. When a mortal breaks <i>Divine Law</i>, the Gods actually can sort it all out. When Agamemnon is murdered by his wife and her lover, his son, Orestes, kills his mother and her lover. This is totally in violation of Divine Law, no killing your parents, bad you. The Furies arrive on the scene, and then everything gets complicated. See, Apollo had ordered him to kill his mother for killing his father, so now everything just became a jurisdictional mess more or less. So, in the next play in the trilogy, we see this resolve through what's essentially a court case. Apollo and Athena set up a trial for Orestes is given with mortals voting as a jury. Apollo presents his case, the Furies present their case, the jury is tied, and Athena gives a tie-breaking vote to spare him. The furies are a bit miffed their authority just got overruled, but, the pair of them are favored children of Zeus. And then Athena essentially bribes them by giving them a new name, &quot;The Kindly Ones,&quot; essentially going, &quot;Oh you're so nice for sparing him, you are the Kindly Ones now, not the Furies,&quot; and they like the sound of that, so they go along with it. So, you can see from this the Theoi actually taking a hands on approach to things, and making sure stuff is fair. Or, well, fair for a Bronze Age society. Or any human society.<br /> <br /> The Theoi are literally everything, they aren't people, we make them look like people. They're more like abstract notions that we mush into the shapes of people to understand them better, potentially why shapeshifting is such a big part of Greek mythology. They make sure the laws work, they make sure people are born, and that people die. (since when people <i>stop</i> dying in the myth of Thanatos' capture, things go funky) They make sure your animals give birth, that your crops grow, that you catch fish, that you come home safely from war, that you become wealthy, that you got married to your childhood sweetheart. They <i>also</i> will slap you for being a shit, offend them, don't venerate them, you know they'll be annoyed and will probably start making your life hard. <i>However</i>, you know that this is pretty much a fair system. You know that if you're a shit, if the King is shit, if a slave is shit, you're all going to get the ire of the Theoi on you. That's a pretty powerful idea, and one that people tend to like, that <i>something</i> is fair, especially when we live in a fundamentally unfair world.<br /> <br /> And, lastly. Why wouldn't the Greeks side with the Titans. Well, as we have seen here, the Theoi are... not awesome, but more or less decent for the ancient Greeks. This is the way it's always been, it would be very similar to someone disliking Gravity, or other natural laws. Secondly, the Golden Age of Cronus is... a debated topic by the Greeks. They were never totally sure one way or another if it was a good thing. It was lawless, there were no cities, there was always food, but essentially there wasn't 'society' on a level we would really get. It was just people, wandering around in the nude, just... being. Zeus arriving, ending this, and giving humanity Law generally gets seen as a good move, people like laws, they're predictable, and they can 'get' them.<br /> <br /> Secondly, the Greeks didn't necessarily side <i>against</i> the Titans, it's just how the world was. You want some good crops? Pray to Demeter, not Cronus. It'd be like filing a complaint with someone who was retired and hadn't worked at the company for a decade, it's pointless, they can't do anything. And you're not about to get them hired back somehow. There would be no point to siding with the Titans, you can't do anything about it, and they (mostly, with exceptions to those not locked up of course) don't have any influence in the world. The interactions between Gods and Humans was because through the Gods, Humans could hope to influence their world around them. They couldn't do that with the imprisoned Titans.<br /> <br /> Thirdly, mortals probably didn't <i>want</i> the Titans breaking free the same reason I don't want people to start tossing Nukes around. These are beings who can hold up the sky, throw mountains, flood the planet, and they <i>fought</i> once. Here, where you live. And everyone <i>died</i>. So, maybe let's not have a gigantic war that kills us all and needs <i>another</i> generation of humans to be made up by whoever emerges the victor. The Age of Iron isn't great, but hey, at least it's not the Age of Lead. The Titans sure as hell aren't going to be nicer to you, they will also think you are annoying gnats. It would just be the same shit with a different set of names, with an apocalypse added in to kill you and everyone you love.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">On the topic of underworld, I've noticed that both the Norse and the Aztec had same ideas about death,that if you die in battle you go to a good afterlife and if you die of old age you go to a afterlife which is not torturous but isnt happy either. Could it be the two cultures have met,long ago?</span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T20:05:44-07:00","id":1148205,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148194#post1148194\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Your average greek hero wasn't a dick though,they were often good people who wanted glory but not into the expense of others. Although the all of them had fatal flaws. Although with that being said,I'm happy scion will let us plays the monsters too,I want to apply the themes of good old Beast to scion</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah, I'm also excited that 2e Scion will allow us to play as characters who aren't strictly humans. The option to play as sentient objects is also something I'm curious about. There's lots of myths about powerful artifacts with a will of their own that we could draw inspiration from. And I know of at least one story in which Aphrodite brings a sculpture to life.<br /> <br /> <br /> I understand the concept of the Theoi being everything, but I also understand that Cronus was the embodiment of Time itself. I'm trying to process the implications of what that meant for Zeus when he decided to overthrow him. Or the consequences of killing Cronus in the Scion setting. He's described as being the King of Titans, so killing the embodiment of Time would probably fuck up everything beyond any hope of repair.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148204#post1148204\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">On the topic of underworld, I've noticed that both the Norse and the Aztec had same ideas about death,that if you die in battle you go to a good afterlife and if you die of old age you go to a afterlife which is not torturous but isnt happy either. Could it be the two cultures have met,long ago?</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>When your society revolves around capturing and sacrificing thousands of slaves on a yearly basis, you need some kind of motivator to spur the troops onward.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 10-08-2017, 11:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T20:20:39-07:00","id":1148207,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148204#post1148204\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">On the topic of underworld, I've noticed that both the Norse and the Aztec had same ideas about death,that if you die in battle you go to a good afterlife and if you die of old age you go to a afterlife which is not torturous but isnt happy either. Could it be the two cultures have met,long ago?</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>Naw, the Mexica and the Norse had zero contact with each other. Cultures come up with the same ideas as each other all the time, Agriculture was invented several times all across the world. Bows, smithing, animal husbandry, people worked these things out by themselves all across the world.<br /> <br /> Just because two cultures share some concepts doesn't mean they had any contact with each other. The Mexica and the Norse do have ideas of a Warrior Afterlife, but they have pretty much zero similarities asides from that. The Mexica had a whole slew of afterlives, ones for children sacrificed to Tlaloc, one for women who die in childbirth (regarded as equal to men dying in battle), a whole slew of afterlives. The Mexica were becoming a powerhouse in the 14th century, which was several hundred years after the Norse had essentially stopped being Norse and had become Scandinavian, including conversion to Christianity several hundred years beforehand.<br /> <br /> For some other examples, the Ashanti and the Irish both have a term for &quot;Long-Hand,&quot; as sort of a title for a person, but they don't have any cultural contact, they are separated by a thousand years. The Japanese, the Yoruba, the Ashanti, and the Haida all have notions of Divine Possession, but they're not at all connected culturally. (except the Yoruba and the Ashanti who are very physically and chronologically close to each other)<br /> <br /> Essentially, in order to be able to say that two cultural groups had a connection, you need <i>really solid</i> evidence. Back in the Bad Ol' Days of Western Academia, they <i>loved</i> going, &quot;Oh, Cultural-Group-X was <i>clearly</i> influenced by the European-Cultural-Group because of the notion of XYZ,&quot; since they couldn't possibly conceive that Europeans weren't the tip-top best and everyone wanted to copy them. Lots of claiming that African religions were actually just practicing a misunderstood version of Christianity, Greek Religion, or Mesopotamian religion. Due to this, suggestions of cultural connections need to be really iron clad. The Mexica and the Norse don't have any evidence of contact, their timelines don't sync up, no archaeological or literary references, it's a solid bet to say that there was never any contact.<br /> <br /> Now, sometimes there are legitimate cultural connections we can see. The Greeks drew heavily from their surrounding neighbors, the Hittites, the Mesopotamians, and the Canninites. The Indo-European cultures have a <i>thing</i> about cattle and virgins. We have really, really strong evidence of these connections though, including linguistic links, chronological syncing, archaeological findings, all that sort of stuff supporting it.<br /> <br /> Good job seeing connections though! Sorry if that came off a bit heavy, it's one of those things that you want to point out as a Historian after our long history of being a bit shitty on the topic. Desperate to make sure people learn from our own mistakes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148205#post1148205\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I understand the concept of the Theoi being everything, but I also understand that Cronus was the embodiment of Time itself. I'm trying to process the implications of what that meant for Zeus when he decided to overthrow him. Or the consequences of killing Cronus in the Scion setting. He's described as being the King of Titans, so killing the embodiment of Time would probably fuck up everything beyond any hope of repair.</div> </div> </div> </div>Cronus is an Agracultural (probably) entity, Chronos (same root as Chronology, Chronomancy, etc) is the personification of time in early Greek philosophy. Chronos isn't a Titan, he's a philosophical concept. 18th and 19th century Academics totally mess up and identify them as the same entity, and no one really caught on for a while so they're still represented as similar in pop-culture all the time. It's the exact same thing as Susano-O and being a Storm God. Some duty Victorian academic dropped the ball but it sort of got stuck.<br /> <br /> Zeus' reason for overthrowing his father was because his mother wanted him to, and because Cronus had eaten all of his brothers and sisters, and Zeus is a dutiful son who did as his mother and grandmother willed him to. Killing Cronus in Scion would probably result in massive agraculture problems, or perhaps taking away some of the chaos of the world, making the natural world perfectly exactly predictable. Or possibly just taking away that spark of 'doing your own thing' that humans have.<br /> <br /> The other way you can see it is that Cronus was ruling a lawless, chaotic land with Zeus coming in and imposing order on this world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 10-08-2017, 11:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T20:28:24-07:00","id":1148209,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148207#post1148207\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Naw, the Mexica and the Norse had zero contact with each other. Cultures come up with the same ideas as each other all the time, Agriculture was invented several times all across the world. Bows, smithing, animal husbandry, people worked these things out by themselves all across the world.<br /> <br /> Just because two cultures share some concepts doesn't mean they had any contact with each other. The Mexica and the Norse do have ideas of a Warrior Afterlife, but they have pretty much zero similarities asides from that. The Mexica had a whole slew of afterlives, ones for children sacrificed to Tlaloc, one for women who die in childbirth (regarded as equal to men dying in battle), a whole slew of afterlives. The Mexica were becoming a powerhouse in the 14th century, which was several hundred years after the Norse had essentially stopped being Norse and had become Scandinavian, including conversion to Christianity several hundred years beforehand.<br /> <br /> For some other examples, the Ashanti and the Irish both have a term for &quot;Long-Hand,&quot; as sort of a title for a person, but they don't have any cultural contact, they are separated by a thousand years. The Japanese, the Yoruba, the Ashanti, and the Haida all have notions of Divine Possession, but they're not at all connected culturally. (except the Yoruba and the Ashanti who are very physically and chronologically close to each other)<br /> <br /> Essentially, in order to be able to say that two cultural groups had a connection, you need <i>really solid</i> evidence. Back in the Bad Ol' Days of Western Academia, they <i>loved</i> going, &quot;Oh, Cultural-Group-X was <i>clearly</i> influenced by the European-Cultural-Group because of the notion of XYZ,&quot; since they couldn't possibly conceive that Europeans weren't the tip-top best and everyone wanted to copy them. Lots of claiming that African religions were actually just practicing a misunderstood version of Christianity, Greek Religion, or Mesopotamian religion. Due to this, suggestions of cultural connections need to be really iron clad. The Mexica and the Norse don't have any evidence of contact, their timelines don't sync up, no archaeological or literary references, it's a solid bet to say that there was never any contact.<br /> <br /> Now, sometimes there are legitimate cultural connections we can see. The Greeks drew heavily from their surrounding neighbors, the Hittites, the Mesopotamians, and the Canninites. The Indo-European cultures have a <i>thing</i> about cattle and virgins. We have really, really strong evidence of these connections though, including linguistic links, chronological syncing, archaeological findings, all that sort of stuff supporting it.<br /> <br /> Good job seeing connections though! Sorry if that came off a bit heavy, it's one of those things that you want to point out as a Historian after our long history of being a bit shitty on the topic. Desperate to make sure people learn from our own mistakes.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Cronus is an Agracultural (probably) entity, Chronos (same root as Chronology, Chronomancy, etc) is the personification of time in early Greek philosophy. Chronos isn't a Titan, he's a philosophical concept. 18th and 19th century Academics totally mess up and identify them as the same entity, and no one really caught on for a while so they're still represented as similar in pop-culture all the time. It's the exact same thing as Susano-O and being a Storm God. Some duty Victorian academic dropped the ball but it sort of got stuck.<br /> <br /> Zeus' reason for overthrowing his father was because his mother wanted him to, and because Cronus had eaten all of his brothers and sisters, and Zeus is a dutiful son who did as his mother and grandmother willed him to. Killing Cronus in Scion would probably result in massive agraculture problems, or perhaps taking away some of the chaos of the world, making the natural world perfectly exactly predictable. Or possibly just taking away that spark of 'doing your own thing' that humans have.</div> </div> </div> </div>Susano'o is not a storm god? Oh man,and I thought he was so cool in that one comic where he had a thunders sworld, do the kami don't have a storm god then?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T20:37:57-07:00","id":1148212,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148209#post1148209\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Susano'o is not a storm god? Oh man,and I thought he was so cool in that one comic where he had a thunders sworld, do the kami don't have a storm god then?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Raiden's a pretty angry lightning god. He has a tendency to eat children's stomachs when he's in a bad mood. I wouldn't put it past him to whip up a storm every now and then.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T20:39:27-07:00","id":1148213,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148209#post1148209\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Susano'o is not a storm god? Oh man,and I thought he was so cool in that one comic where he had a thunders sworld, do the kami don't have a storm god then?</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh yeah, they totally have a Storm God who is <i>crazy awesome</i>. His name is Takemikazuchi, he conquered The Land of Plentiful Reeds (Earth) for Amaterasu, and conquered the Earthly Kami to bring them under the influence of Amaterasu's court. He defeated a gigantic earthquake causing catfish, and makes sure he stays subdued every month of the year save for when the Kami are all stuck in a month long meeting of the entire Pantheon. If you want to read more about him, you can check out his entry in the revised Pantheon I wrote along with Sacerdos right <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1nn03gHFj_tgASkDPGbI1FgtTBff88OrgioSJzyIvEbY/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>!<br /> <br /> The idea that Susano-O is a storm Deity was due to one of the earliest translations of the Japanese imperial religious texts. Essentially, the academic who did the translation looked at Amaterasu and saw &quot;Sun Goddess,&quot; looked at Tsukuyomi and saw &quot;Moon God,&quot; and then looked at Susano-O and saw the text said he was put incharge of the oceans, and went, &quot;Ah, but he <i>has</i> to have a celestial component like his siblings, so he must be all about storms and that's why it talks about the ocean here.&quot; missing the fact that Susano-O <i>refuses</i> his authority over the ocean and gets banished by his father for refusing his position and goes off to sulk about it.<br /> <br /> It's all one big misunderstanding based on this early translation. Funny fact about that translation, the guy who did it translated all the sections he found 'risque' in Latin hoping that any women reading his translation wouldn't be able to read the sections he deemed inappropriate for them due to sexual content. Oh Victorian Academia, you are so weird, gross, and annoying at times.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T21:10:40-07:00","id":1148217,"title":"","content":"My working headcanon for Scion right now is the default state of the Underworld, as one vast dimension divided into different territories, is bleak and desolate - but some gods however create paradise pockets in them through death &amp; rebirth cycles bringing life/light/etc to the Underworld. Osiris' death &amp; resurrection, followed by underworld enthronement created A'aru. Persephone's annual trips gradually grew a garden touched by her experiences in the world above that became Elysium. Etc.<br /> <br /> That's why earlier and later Greek myth are so different with regard to the Underworld. It was originally utterly desolate, but Persephone gradually changed it over time. This song does a good job capturing the role Persephone plays with regard to her husband's subjects: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-7YKDgkVWvo\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-7YKDgkVWvo</a><br /> <br /> (note: Valhalla is not an underworld paradise; the Valkyries are just bringing select souls to Asgard itself)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-08T21:29:16-07:00","id":1148218,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148217#post1148217\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My working headcanon for Scion right now is the default state of the Underworld, as one vast dimension divided into different territories, is bleak and desolate - but some gods however create paradise pockets in them through death &amp; rebirth cycles bringing life/light/etc to the Underworld. Osiris' death &amp; resurrection, followed by underworld enthronement created A'aru. Persephone's annual trips gradually grew a garden touched by her experiences in the world above that became Elysium. Etc.<br /> <br /> That's why earlier and later Greek myth are so different with regard to the Underworld. It was originally utterly desolate, but Persephone gradually changed it over time. This song does a good job capturing the role Persephone plays with regard to her husband's subjects: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-7YKDgkVWvo\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-7YKDgkVWvo</a><br /> <br /> (note: Valhalla is not an underworld paradise; the Valkyries are just bringing select souls to Asgard itself)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> That's kind of what the Over / Under Worlds are. They're all part of a vast, infinite space that the various pantheons found a place to set down roots and build their territories. When a new pantheon emerges, they locate an unoccupied spot in this expanse of infinity and build their realms from scratch.<br /> <br /> In the Scion cosmology, you can actually walk from Olympus to Asguard if you were so inclined. Might take you centuries to do so, but it is still physically possible, because they exist within the same plane of reality as each other. The original Tartatus was located in the center of each pantheon's Underworld, which were built around the outside of it like spokes on a wheel.<br /> <br /> When the Titans broke free, it scattered their Underworlds, so now they're isolated from each other.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 10-09-2017, 12:35 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-09T00:09:06-07:00","id":1148234,"title":"","content":"I'm aware of the continuous nature of the respective reality layers, at least in 1E (not clear at all yet that that is the case in 2E). My point was that I interpreted the Underworld as inherently desolate and it being the dying/reborn chthonic gods who brought life to it in pockets like Aaru and Elysium.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-09T17:02:52-07:00","id":1148473,"title":"","content":"To be fair on the Kronus/Chronos conflation, that does date back to late antiquity, so if Scion 2E ends up embracing it, I\u2019d be fine with that. I certainly see time as being in the mix for Kronus\u2019 domain along with reaping/harvest/fruition. I don\u2019t know which would be primary for him as a Titan, but Fertility, Stars &amp; Death would all definitely be in the mix for the old guy with the scythe (the originating image of \u201cFather Time\u201d).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-09T17:44:24-07:00","id":1148480,"title":"","content":"First, to directly address the topic this thread is about, in my 1E games, I regularly had the discovery and/or creation of God-slaying weapons. This is basically a magical alloy that reacts poorly to the presence of ichor, thus making it more brutal to those of higher Legend. I can't recall off the top of my head what I used as the ingredients for it, but I figured that the basic premise could still be useful. It was always rare, though, and usually created by Ixion. If obtained in large amounts, it could be quite dangerous in the hands of mortals. Of course, your average God won't just stand there and let you take a swing at them with it, so it wasn't exactly an &quot;I win&quot; button, but it definitely helped.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148185#post1148185\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If that's the case, then why the hell aren't mortals siding with the Titans against the Theoi?</div> </div> </div> </div>Why would they automatically assume that things would be better with them? The Titans overthrew the previous generation, then tried to keep the next generation down. The Theoi weren't seen as these horrible usurpers that overthrew the noble Titans so that they could stomp all over humanity. The overthrow of the Titans was seen as a good thing in Greek mythology. Sure, Prometheus seemed to be on our side, but how many of the others favored humankind?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148185#post1148185\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Greek Underworld is pretty frickin depressing. Only the best of the best get to go to any thing remotely similar to heaven (Elysian Fields) and you need to do that 3 consecutive times before you're allowed to retire on the <i>Isles of the Blessed</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>This assumes that A) the ancient Greeks would have had the thought that a heavenly afterlife for everyone was even possible and B) that all you had to do to get into it was start worshiping different Gods. I don't believe that heavenly afterlives were that common in the various mythologies of the world. Hell, I don't even think souls going to Heaven came into Christianity until a bit later on, though I'd need to track down my sources for that. I believe that early Judeo-Christian teachings said that we basically slumbered within our bodies until the end of the world, when we'd be bodily resurrected. I think even the doctrine of the soul was adopted from some of the Greek philosophers. People who assume that the afterlife works a certain way are unlikely to think otherwise just because they encounter someone who believes something different. If the ancient Greek hears someone saying, &quot;Come worship my God! Forsake yours, and you will have an eternal paradise after you die,&quot; they wouldn't necessarily be able to just toss aside everything they grew up with and embrace this novel idea. They'd probably be more likely to think, &quot;Yeah, sure. I can go and worship your God, and then when I die, my Gods will be mad at me for neglecting them on top of everything else. No thank you.&quot; Even today, afterlife situation is not necessarily the best way to go shopping for religion. People generally seem to believe that the afterlife follows the same rules for everyone, regardless of their belief system. I don't hear a lot of people saying, &quot;Why would you want to reincarnate when you could come to Heaven with me instead?&quot; I'm more likely to hear someone saying &quot;burn in Hell for all eternity&quot; than &quot;reincarnate&quot; in that sentence. Someone needs to believe in a given afterlife before they can begin worrying about how they're going to deal with it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148185#post1148185\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I used to be a huge fan of Greek mythology. But the more I learn about them, the more I realize they're a bunch of giant dicks! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>The Gods did good things for people, too. If you go to <a href=\"https://www.Theoi.com\" target=\"_blank\">www.Theoi.com</a>, you'll see that they also gave blessings to many individuals. It wasn't all wrath, and even some of the wrath incidents could be seen as totally justified. Just look at Lycaon. I'm sure if we could look at it through the ancient Greek cultural lens, even more of the wrath would probably seem just.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148185#post1148185\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only one who was even remotely worthy of respect was Hades, and the only dickish thing he ever did was against a fellow deity. I mean, unless the mortal intentionally picked a fight with him...</div> </div> </div> </div>Really? Okay, maybe my memory's a bit foggy. What did Hestia do that rendered her unworthy of respect? Iris? Hebe? While I agree that Hades deserves a lot more respect than he's given in the modern day, I find it odd to say that he's the only one deserving of it. Of course, I respect them all, so that's to be expected.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148193#post1148193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Gods being dicks is pretty universal among ancient pantheons. It\u2019s not the case with (for example) modern Hindu deities because said religion kept up with moral evolution of civilization, instead of us just looking at an ancient snapshot of cultural values as with a long dead belief system.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. Thank you. While I disagree about it being a long dead belief system, there definitely something to be said about the evolution of myth over time, especially in relation to the mortal culture around it. Even the Christian God was depicted as being pretty dickish in the early texts, but most of the believers in him today don't look at him that way. How different would these myths and the depictions of the Gods be today if they had remained at the center of major cultures for an extra 2000 years?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Jason Ross Inczauskis</b>, Freelance Writer<br /> Projects: <i>Dark Eras 2</i>, <i>Mummy: The Curse 2e, Book of Lasting Death, DtR The Clades Companion, Pirates of Pugmire,</i> <i>They Came From Beyond the Grave!, </i><i>TC Aeon: Mission Statements</i>, <i>TC In Media Res,</i> <i>DtD Night Horrors: Enemy Action,</i> <i>C20 Anthology of Dreams</i><br /> Masculine pronouns preferred.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/309-white-oak-dragon","name":"White Oak Dragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-09T18:12:14-07:00","id":1148484,"title":"","content":"Oh, I understand the concept that different cultures had different ideologies back then. I've already come to the realization that laws and justice are subjective concepts that change according to whoever's in power at the time. But still, you'd think that at least some people might have preferred Titans running the show, as opposed to what they traded them in for.<br /> <br /> The Titans kept humanity down? Because I heard the Theoi did pretty much the same thing. And from the sounds of it, they did it a heck of a lot more cruelly. I mean, sure, humanity was small in number and didn't really have any drive to progress themselves. But that's because we didn't need to do so. We were immortal, and there was no hunger, disease or anything that might negatively impact us. What's the point of trying to improve your life if you already live in a state of utopia?<br /> <br /> <br /> The reason Zeus denied us the gift of fire is because he accidentally chose the wrong sacrifices. Basically, he told mortals that they get to keep all the good stuff, while the gods survived off our table scraps. He had first pick of the draw, he chose poorly, and decided to punish all of us because he was a sore loser.<br /> <br /> Actually, there is quite a lot of mythology in which the Theoi outright lose in competitions and decide to go on a rampage like a bunch of spoiled brats. Some might say that the mortals challenging the Gods is a sign of hubris, but I say that it's not 'hubris' if it's true.<br /> <br /> <br /> As for Hestia, Ibis and the like, I've barely ever heard of them, so I don't know who they are or what they're about. And I thought Iris was a goddess of chaos, or at least she was in possession of an apple that produced chaos. Although I might be thinking of a different figure, since I've heard it pronounced as &quot;Eris.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> --------------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> Anyways, the ideas of creating a weapon in game is a viable option. It might not have been the mythological inspiration I was hoping for, but I could totally see it being a possibility. The game takes place in modern times, with technology the people of the old world had never even dreamed of. So creating new methods specifically designed to combat the Pantheons who defeated them last time might be a priority for the Titans and their servants.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 10-09-2017, 09:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-09T20:11:51-07:00","id":1148504,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148484#post1148484\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Titans kept humanity down? Because I heard the Theoi did pretty much the same thing. And from the sounds of it, they did it a heck of a lot more cruelly. I mean, sure, humanity was small in number and didn't really have any drive to progress themselves. But that's because we didn't need to do so. We were immortal, and there was no hunger, disease or anything that might negatively impact us. What's the point of trying to improve your life if you already live in a state of utopia?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's... kind of more complicated then that. A good way to look at it is that the Golden Age Humans were essentially just animals. No culture, no traditions, no cities. No music, no poetry, no nothing. It was you, naked, in a forest. You could pluck apples from trees and eat your fill, but, that was life. You couldn't get sick and die, but you also had no culture, traditions, or connections with each other. It wasn't just lawless, it was existing without definition. There were no such things as familial connections, familial love, it was just you, in a forest, with fruit. Might have been nice fruit, but there was nothing else. <br /> <br /> It was a Golden Age in that there was no sickness, death, or bad stuff. But there also wasn't necessarily good stuff. It was a paradise, but, it was also kinda meh. We don't have much record of humanity during this period, but the Theoi do actually bless these first humans with minor divinity. These Golden Age humans become the Deamones, minor spirits that make the world work.<br /> <br /> Further, the Greeks didn't believe they were the same as Golden Age humans. Each 'Age' of humanity is more like a different species. The Silver Age humans were a bunch of dicks. The Heroic Age humans were a bit hit and miss. Iron is the least powerful, weakest Age, but at the same time there's a great lack of giants coming around ruining your day.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148484#post1148484\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The reason Zeus denied us the gift of fire is because he accidentally chose the wrong sacrifices. Basically, he told mortals that they get to keep all the good stuff, while the gods survived off our table scraps. He had first pick of the draw, he chose poorly, and decided to punish all of us because he was a sore loser.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not quite how it goes. Prometheus (probably due to being mad with Zeus over the treatment of his brother Atlas and his father Idapeus) goes to humanity, and teaches them how to trick the Gods out of their part of sacrifices. Zeus didn't accidentally chose the wrong sacrifice, Prometheus tricked us into tricking the Gods (keep in mind that Prometheus is the Titan of Foresight, and thus likely has a good idea of the outcome of his actions) and Zeus took away fire to both punish us, and prevent Prometheus' trick from having any effect. Fire was the medium of sacrifice, the Greeks primarily used burned offerings for the heavenly Gods. (Then Prometheus steals back fire and gives it to humanity since Zeus spoiled his trick, etc etc.) We benefited from all of this, but the big thing to keep in mind is that all of these entities treat humanity like pawns in their game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148484#post1148484\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for Hestia, Ibis and the like, I've barely ever heard of them, so I don't know who they are or what they're about. And I thought Iris was a goddess of chaos, or at least she was in possession of an apple that produced chaos. Although I might be thinking of a different figure, since I've heard it pronounced as &quot;Eris.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Hestia is one of the Major Gods of the Greek and Roman Pantheons, the Goddess of the Hearth, and one of the original six children of Cronus and Rhea. She is exceptionally nice, watches over humanity and families from their hearths, is generally very helpful. We don't have a ton of myths of her since she's more of an everyday religious entity rather than a grand mythological entity. Very important though. She is originally one of the Twelve Gods of Olympus, but gives up her throne to young Dionysus when he returns to Olympus.<br /> <br /> Iris is the Goddess of the Rainbow, and is generally seen as a rather nice lady. She's the other messenger of the Gods, going hither and tither to relay prayers and information between Olympus and earth. She's a Minor Goddess, but a pretty nice lady. <br /> <br /> Hebe is a Goddess of Childhood, daughter of Zeus and Hera, married to Heracles to make up for the whole 'situation' between himself and Hera after his Apotheosis. Also a rather nice person.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-09T21:42:22-07:00","id":1148518,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148504#post1148504\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's... kind of more complicated then that. A good way to look at it is that the Golden Age Humans were essentially just animals. No culture, no traditions, no cities. No music, no poetry, no nothing. It was you, naked, in a forest. You could pluck apples from trees and eat your fill, but, that was life. You couldn't get sick and die, but you also had no culture, traditions, or connections with each other. It wasn't just lawless, it was existing without definition. There were no such things as familial connections, familial love, it was just you, in a forest, with fruit. Might have been nice fruit, but there was nothing else.<br /> <br /> It was a Golden Age in that there was no sickness, death, or bad stuff. But there also wasn't necessarily good stuff. It was a paradise, but, it was also kinda meh. We don't have much record of humanity during this period, but the Theoi do actually bless these first humans with minor divinity. These Golden Age humans become the Deamones, minor spirits that make the world work.<br /> <br /> Further, the Greeks didn't believe they were the same as Golden Age humans. Each 'Age' of humanity is more like a different species. The Silver Age humans were a bunch of dicks. The Heroic Age humans were a bit hit and miss. Iron is the least powerful, weakest Age, but at the same time there's a great lack of giants coming around ruining your day.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> That's not quite how it goes. Prometheus (probably due to being mad with Zeus over the treatment of his brother Atlas and his father Idapeus) goes to humanity, and teaches them how to trick the Gods out of their part of sacrifices. Zeus didn't accidentally chose the wrong sacrifice, Prometheus tricked us into tricking the Gods (keep in mind that Prometheus is the Titan of Foresight, and thus likely has a good idea of the outcome of his actions) and Zeus took away fire to both punish us, and prevent Prometheus' trick from having any effect. Fire was the medium of sacrifice, the Greeks primarily used burned offerings for the heavenly Gods. (Then Prometheus steals back fire and gives it to humanity since Zeus spoiled his trick, etc etc.) We benefited from all of this, but the big thing to keep in mind is that all of these entities treat humanity like pawns in their game.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Hestia is one of the Major Gods of the Greek and Roman Pantheons, the Goddess of the Hearth, and one of the original six children of Cronus and Rhea. She is exceptionally nice, watches over humanity and families from their hearths, is generally very helpful. We don't have a ton of myths of her since she's more of an everyday religious entity rather than a grand mythological entity. Very important though. She is originally one of the Twelve Gods of Olympus, but gives up her throne to young Dionysus when he returns to Olympus.<br /> <br /> Iris is the Goddess of the Rainbow, and is generally seen as a rather nice lady. She's the other messenger of the Gods, going hither and tither to relay prayers and information between Olympus and earth. She's a Minor Goddess, but a pretty nice lady.<br /> <br /> Hebe is a Goddess of Childhood, daughter of Zeus and Hera, married to Heracles to make up for the whole 'situation' between himself and Hera after his Apotheosis. Also a rather nice person.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, there <i>used to be</i> a distinct lack of giants. But, you know, Titans escaped and what not. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> Yeah, I know about the fire being part of the sacrifices deal, but getting tricked was Zeus' fault. They presented two mystery bags in front of him and in his greed, he chose the one that he thought had the best offering. Nobody lied to him about which bag was better, he just assumed that his choice was the one he wanted. And when it turned out that wasn't the case, he threw a fit.<br /> <br /> <br /> Thanks for clearing up the other goddesses for me, though. Hestia's job sounds like what I was lead to believe Hera was in charge of. I know she's a goddess of marriage, but I thought hearth and home were also part of her gig. It's been years since I read any Greek stories, and I don't exactly have the best memory. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-10T03:05:42-07:00","id":1148549,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148518#post1148518\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, there <i>used to be</i> a distinct lack of giants. But, you know, Titans escaped and what not. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Giants are not looking at a happy ending here. They betrayed the Titans and fought with the gods, then betrayed the gods and lost. So pretty much everybody hates them now, outside of fringe exceptions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2447-twitchtherat","name":"TwitchTheRat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2447_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-10T04:59:13-07:00","id":1148554,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TwitchTheRat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148549#post1148549\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Giants are not looking at a happy ending here. They betrayed the Titans and fought with the gods, then betrayed the gods and lost. So pretty much everybody hates them now, outside of fringe exceptions.</div> </div> </div> </div>I\u2019m not aware of a portrayal of the Gigantes as having fought the Titans with the Gods. Are you possibly conflating them with the Hekatonchire and Elder Cyclopes?<br /> <br /> Gaia herself supported the Gods against the Titans but then was furious with the Titans receiving the same imprisonment she was mad over with the Hekatonchire and Cyclopes so then turned on the Gods with the Gigantes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-10T06:46:49-07:00","id":1148565,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TwitchTheRat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148549#post1148549\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Giants are not looking at a happy ending here. They betrayed the Titans and fought with the gods, then betrayed the gods and lost. So pretty much everybody hates them now, outside of fringe exceptions.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The Ragnarok prophecy tells a different story. Giants are Fated to win the war and destroy everything. The Frost Giants conquer the World for at least 10 whole years, then the Fire Giants lay siege to Asgard and burn it to the ground. Even if we do end up repopulating after all is said and done, the Giants still end up being the winning team.<br /> <br /> <br /> You can kill the Gods largely without consequences; they usually just end up having a replacement take over the position. But you kill a Titan, and it has traumatic effects on the World itself, most of which are irreversible. It's a war of attrition, and the odds are stacked in the Titan's favor. So any Scions who join them may not be as crazy as they sound.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 10-10-2017, 09:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-10T08:07:29-07:00","id":1148588,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148565#post1148565\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Ragnarok prophecy tells a different story. Giants are Fated to win the war and destroy everything. The Frost Giants conquer the World for at least 10 whole years, then the Fire Giants lay siege to Asgard and burn it to the ground. Even if we do end up repopulating after all is said and done, the Giants still end up being the winning team.</div> </div> </div> </div>Twitch is talking about the Gigantes, the Giants of Greece that were born armed in gleaming bronze from the blood of Ouranos' severed genitals, not the Norse Jotun.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-10T08:17:38-07:00","id":1148589,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148565#post1148565\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> The Ragnarok prophecy tells a different story. Giants are Fated to win the war and destroy everything. The Frost Giants conquer the World for at least 10 whole years, then the Fire Giants lay siege to Asgard and burn it to the ground. Even if we do end up repopulating after all is said and done, the Giants still end up being the winning team.</div> </div> </div> </div>Surtr maybe since he\u2019s just a force of destruction, but the Jotun end up as dead as the gods do. They don\u2019t win. If anything the surviving/resurrecting handful of gods means the Aesir are in a slightly better position at the end of Ragnarok than the jotun, with a fertile new earth and a new man and woman to begin fresh.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-10T08:54:53-07:00","id":1148600,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148589#post1148589\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Surtr maybe since he\u2019s just a force of destruction, but the Jotun end up as dead as the gods do. They don\u2019t win. If anything the surviving/resurrecting handful of gods means the Aesir are in a slightly better position at the end of Ragnarok than the jotun, with a fertile new earth and a new man and woman to begin fresh.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I actually wondered what the fate of the Jotun turned out to be, since I never found anything specifically detailing it. The Ragnarok thing just goes on to say they kill all the gods, then two survivors repopulate the earth. Giants just kind of vanished from the story, as far as I could tell.<br /> <br /> Well, they aren't 'new' men and women. They're part of the current pantheon, who just happened to take shelter inside the World Tree...<br /> <br /> Which the Muspel are supposed to set fire to...<br /> <br /> How exactly did they survive that? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-10T11:27:51-07:00","id":1148641,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148600#post1148600\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I actually wondered what the fate of the Jotun turned out to be, since I never found anything specifically detailing it. The Ragnarok thing just goes on to say they kill all the gods, then two survivors repopulate the earth. Giants just kind of vanished from the story, as far as I could tell.<br /> <br /> Well, they aren't 'new' men and women. They're part of the current pantheon, who just happened to take shelter inside the World Tree...<br /> <br /> Which the Muspel are supposed to set fire to...<br /> <br /> How exactly did they survive that? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Lif and Lifthrasir are mortals from my understanding. As for the world tree, it gets damaged, but the Eddas make it clear that its always existed and always will. No matter how much damage Nidhogg, the deer or the other critters living in it do to it, its always refreshed by the Norns (who presumably survive Ragnarok as they aren't mentioned at all really from what I remember) with the waters of the Well of Urd.<br /> <br /> Ragnarok leaves plenty of room to get creative. We know the Aesir win in the end despite all the losses, but we don't really know for sure the giants were wiped out completely, we don't have a clue about the Alfar and the Vanir presumably don't take part at all, even Njord ditches the Aesir to return to Vanaheim before it all hits the fan. On top of all that, the goddesses are hardly mentioned at all. If Frigg is mourning Odin, does that mean she survived? Where was Freyja who had the other half of the einherjar? If Hel sent people to aid her father, presumably that means she didn't go herself, so is she still around? A lot of ideas to play around with.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13472-~nkf~","name":"~NKF~","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-10T13:38:40-07:00","id":1148668,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148554#post1148554\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I\u2019m not aware of a portrayal of the Gigantes as having fought the Titans with the Gods. Are you possibly conflating them with the Hekatonchire and Elder Cyclopes?</div> </div> </div> </div>The giants were children of Ouranos and Gaea, and included the cyclopes among them, though some of them were exceptions that had different parents, like Polyphemus. The Hecatoncheires are basically badass famous giants, like the giant equivalent of Hercales and Achilles. The Gigantes were a specific group of giants that participated in the Gigantomachy, but they were all giants. That's why the gods considered it such a betrayal when things went bad during the Gigantomachy, because the giants had been on their side previously.<br /> <br /> I mean, that's racial profiling. Just because some giants helped once upon a time doesn't mean you are buddies with all giants, but that's mythology for you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2447,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2447-twitchtherat","user":"TwitchTheRat","date":". 10-10-2017, 04:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2447-twitchtherat","name":"TwitchTheRat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2447_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-10T14:19:27-07:00","id":1148680,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TwitchTheRat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148668#post1148668\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The giants were children of Ouranos and Gaea, and included the cyclopes among them, though some of them were exceptions that had different parents, like Polyphemus. The Hecatoncheires are basically badass famous giants, like the giant equivalent of Hercales and Achilles. The Gigantes were a specific group of giants that participated in the Gigantomachy, but they were all giants. That's why the gods considered it such a betrayal when things went bad during the Gigantomachy, because the giants had been on their side previously.<br /> <br /> I mean, that's racial profiling. Just because some giants helped once upon a time doesn't mean you are buddies with all giants, but that's mythology for you.</div> </div> </div> </div>You\u2019re classifying things together under the label \u201cgiants\u201d that as far as I am aware no Greek writers ever classified together. The English word \u201cgiants\u201d derives from gigantes. There is no Greek word that was applied to Gigantes, Cyclopes, and Hekatonchire collectively. <br /> <br /> I\u2019m not aware of any Greek myth narrative that has the gods seeing the Cyclopes as traitors because of the actions of the Gigantes. Except for Apollo, who took out his fury over the death of Asclepius on the cyclopes\u2019 for making Zeus\u2019 thunder bolt (because he couldn\u2019t take it out on Zeus), the gods generally had a good relationship with the cyclopes. Zeus even severely punished Apollo for his actions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-10T15:19:47-07:00","id":1148694,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148680#post1148680\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is no Greek word that was applied to Gigantes, Cyclopes, and Hekatonchire collectively.</div> </div> </div> </div>They were the same species, roughly. Every single one of them were described as giants, not just in stature but in overall similar characteristics, except the Hecatoncheires who were giants taken up to 11.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2447-twitchtherat","name":"TwitchTheRat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2447_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-10T15:22:25-07:00","id":1148695,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TwitchTheRat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148694#post1148694\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They were the same species, roughly. Every single one of them were described as giants, not just in stature but in overall similar characteristics, except the Hecatoncheires who were giants taken up to 11.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> What characteristics were those?<br /> <br /> I'm guessing they were at least roughly humanoid in shape?<br /> <br /> On an unrelated note, I'm curious as to why a race of beings known for their great skill and craftsmanship are often portrayed as being stupid. I'd classify them more as being savants, if that were the case.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uGSSHiILbVM\" target=\"_blank\">There are different kinds of intelligence besides book smarts.</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 10-10-2017, 06:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-10T16:28:23-07:00","id":1148702,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TwitchTheRat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1147785-godly-kryptonite?p=1148694#post1148694\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They were the same species, roughly. Every single one of them were described as giants, not just in stature but in overall similar characteristics, except the Hecatoncheires who were giants taken up to 11.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think there might be a misunderstanding here due to translations. Yes, the Elder Cyclopes, the Hecatoncheires, the Gigantes, and the Younger Cyclopes are all 'Giant' as an Adjective, but they're not all Giants as a Noun. The Gigantes are Giants (Noun) who are Giant (Adjective), and the Hecatoncheires are Giant (Adjective) but not Giants (Noun). Since English uses essentially the same word for the Noun as the Adjective, it causes a lot of confusion. But, helpfully we can riddle this out with ease checking the original text! And even more helpfully, <a href=\"http://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0129%3Acard%3D1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> is a digital copy of The Theogony that you can click on words for their translation!<br /> <br /> So, we can see that on line 185 we get this, &quot;\u03b3\u03b5\u03af\u03bd\u03b1\u03c4\u1fbd \u1f18\u03c1\u03b9\u03bd\u1fe6\u03c2 \u03c4\u03b5 \u03ba\u03c1\u03b1\u03c4\u03b5\u03c1\u1f70\u03c2 \u03bc\u03b5\u03b3\u03ac\u03bb\u03bf\u03c5\u03c2 \u03c4\u03b5 \u0393\u03af\u03b3\u03b1\u03bd\u03c4\u03b1\u03c2,&quot; which is translated into English (using <a href=\"http://www.sacred-texts.com/cla/hesiod/theogony.htm\" target=\"_blank\">this</a> translation since my physical one is at home) as, &quot;the strong Erinyes and the great Giants,&quot; with \u0393\u03af\u03b3\u03b1\u03bd\u03c4\u03b1\u03c2 being more specifically the name of The Gigantes. This is Giant (Noun).<br /> <br /> Now, looking to lines regarding the Elder Cyclopes, lines 139 to about 147 in our original text, get this: &quot;\u03b3\u03b5\u03af\u03bd\u03b1\u03c4\u03bf \u03b4\u1fbd \u03b1\u1f56 \u039a\u03cd\u03ba\u03bb\u03c9\u03c0\u03b1\u03c2 \u1f51\u03c0\u03ad\u03c1\u03b2\u03b9\u03bf\u03bd \u1f26\u03c4\u03bf\u03c1 \u1f14\u03c7\u03bf\u03bd\u03c4\u03b1\u03c2, \u0392\u03c1\u03cc\u03bd\u03c4\u03b7\u03bd \u03c4\u03b5 \u03a3\u03c4\u03b5\u03c1\u03cc\u03c0\u03b7\u03bd \u03c4\u03b5 \u03ba\u03b1\u1f76 \u1f0c\u03c1\u03b3\u03b7\u03bd \u1f40\u03b2\u03c1\u03b9\u03bc\u03cc\u03b8\u03c5\u03bc\u03bf\u03bd, \u03bf\u1f33 \u0396\u03b7\u03bd\u1f76 \u03b2\u03c1\u03bf\u03bd\u03c4\u03ae\u03bd \u03c4\u03b5 \u03b4\u03cc\u03c3\u03b1\u03bd \u03c4\u03b5\u1fe6\u03be\u03ac\u03bd \u03c4\u03b5 \u03ba\u03b5\u03c1\u03b1\u03c5\u03bd\u03cc\u03bd. \u03bf\u1f33 \u03b4\u03ae \u03c4\u03bf\u03b9 \u03c4\u1f70 \u03bc\u1f72\u03bd \u1f04\u03bb\u03bb\u03b1 \u03b8\u03b5\u03bf\u1fd6\u03c2 \u1f10\u03bd\u03b1\u03bb\u03af\u03b3\u03ba\u03b9\u03bf\u03b9 \u1f26\u03c3\u03b1\u03bd, \u03bc\u03bf\u1fe6\u03bd\u03bf\u03c2 \u03b4\u1fbd \u1f40\u03c6\u03b8\u03b1\u03bb\u03bc\u1f78\u03c2 \u03bc\u03ad\u03c3\u03c3\u1ff3 \u1f10\u03bd\u03ad\u03ba\u03b5\u03b9\u03c4\u03bf \u03bc\u03b5\u03c4\u03ce\u03c0\u1ff3. \u039a\u03cd\u03ba\u03bb\u03c9\u03c0\u03b5\u03c2 \u03b4\u1fbd \u1f44\u03bd\u03bf\u03bc\u1fbd \u1f26\u03c3\u03b1\u03bd \u1f10\u03c0\u03ce\u03bd\u03c5\u03bc\u03bf\u03bd, \u03bf\u1f55\u03bd\u03b5\u03ba\u1fbd \u1f04\u03c1\u03b1 \u03c3\u03c6\u03ad\u03c9\u03bd \u03ba\u03c5\u03ba\u03bb\u03bf\u03c4\u03b5\u03c1\u1f74\u03c2 \u1f40\u03c6\u03b8\u03b1\u03bb\u03bc\u1f78\u03c2 \u1f15\u03b5\u03b9\u03c2 \u1f10\u03bd\u03ad\u03ba\u03b5\u03b9\u03c4\u03bf \u03bc\u03b5\u03c4\u03ce\u03c0\u1ff3: \u1f30\u03c3\u03c7\u1f7a\u03c2 \u03b4\u1fbd \u1f20\u03b4\u1f72 \u03b2\u03af\u03b7 \u03ba\u03b1\u1f76 \u03bc\u03b7\u03c7\u03b1\u03bd\u03b1\u1f76 \u1f26\u03c3\u03b1\u03bd \u1f10\u03c0\u1fbd \u1f14\u03c1\u03b3\u03bf\u03b9\u03c2.&quot; which connects in our translation as: &quot;And again, she bare the Cyclopes, overbearing in spirit, Brontes, and Steropes and stubborn-hearted Arges (6), who gave Zeus the thunder and made the thunderbolt: in all else they were like the gods, but one eye only was set in the midst of their fore-heads. And they were surnamed Cyclopes (Orb-eyed) because one orbed eye was set in their foreheads. Strength and might and craft were in their works.&quot;<br /> <br /> So, the words in this passage that are important to us is the Noun given to describe these beings, &quot;\u039a\u03cd\u03ba\u03bb\u03c9\u03c8&quot; as far as I can tell, meaning Round Eyed, which connects with the translation of Cyclopes given in our translation as Orb-Eyed there in the second last sentence. There's a total lack of description of stature of these beings even, instead they are described as being similar to the Gods, &quot;\u03b8\u03b5\u03bf\u1fd6\u03c2 \u1f10\u03bd\u03b1\u03bb\u03af\u03b3\u03ba\u03b9\u03bf\u03b9,&quot; rather than any of the large-of-stature beings. <br /> <br /> Then we have the description of the Hecatoncheires, which is lines 147 - 153: &quot;\u1f04\u03bb\u03bb\u03bf\u03b9 \u03b4\u1fbd \u03b1\u1f56 \u0393\u03b1\u03af\u03b7\u03c2 \u03c4\u03b5 \u03ba\u03b1\u1f76 \u039f\u1f50\u03c1\u03b1\u03bd\u03bf\u1fe6 \u1f10\u03be\u03b5\u03b3\u03ad\u03bd\u03bf\u03bd\u03c4\u03bf \u03c4\u03c1\u03b5\u1fd6\u03c2 \u03c0\u03b1\u1fd6\u03b4\u03b5\u03c2 \u03bc\u03b5\u03b3\u03ac\u03bb\u03bf\u03b9 \u03c4\u03b5 \u03ba\u03b1\u1f76 \u1f44\u03b2\u03c1\u03b9\u03bc\u03bf\u03b9, \u03bf\u1f50\u03ba \u1f40\u03bd\u03bf\u03bc\u03b1\u03c3\u03c4\u03bf\u03af, \u039a\u03cc\u03c4\u03c4\u03bf\u03c2 \u03c4\u03b5 \u0392\u03c1\u03b9\u03ac\u03c1\u03b5\u03ce\u03c2 \u03c4\u03b5 \u0393\u03cd\u03b7\u03c2 \u03b8\u1fbd, \u1f51\u03c0\u03b5\u03c1\u03ae\u03c6\u03b1\u03bd\u03b1 \u03c4\u03ad\u03ba\u03bd\u03b1. \u03c4\u1ff6\u03bd \u1f11\u03ba\u03b1\u03c4\u1f78\u03bd \u03bc\u1f72\u03bd \u03c7\u03b5\u1fd6\u03c1\u03b5\u03c2 \u1f00\u03c0\u1fbd \u1f64\u03bc\u03c9\u03bd \u1f00\u03af\u03c3\u03c3\u03bf\u03bd\u03c4\u03bf, \u1f04\u03c0\u03bb\u03b1\u03c3\u03c4\u03bf\u03b9, \u03ba\u03b5\u03c6\u03b1\u03bb\u03b1\u1f76 \u03b4\u1f72 \u1f11\u03ba\u03ac\u03c3\u03c4\u1ff3 \u03c0\u03b5\u03bd\u03c4\u03ae\u03ba\u03bf\u03bd\u03c4\u03b1 \u1f10\u03be \u1f64\u03bc\u03c9\u03bd \u1f10\u03c0\u03ad\u03c6\u03c5\u03ba\u03bf\u03bd \u1f10\u03c0\u1f76 \u03c3\u03c4\u03b9\u03b2\u03b1\u03c1\u03bf\u1fd6\u03c3\u03b9 \u03bc\u03ad\u03bb\u03b5\u03c3\u03c3\u03b9\u03bd: \u1f30\u03c3\u03c7\u1f7a\u03c2 \u03b4\u1fbd \u1f04\u03c0\u03bb\u03b7\u03c4\u03bf\u03c2 \u03ba\u03c1\u03b1\u03c4\u03b5\u03c1\u1f74 \u03bc\u03b5\u03b3\u03ac\u03bb\u1ff3 \u1f10\u03c0\u1f76 \u03b5\u1f34\u03b4\u03b5\u03b9.&quot; which our translation gives as, &quot;And again, three other sons were born of Earth and Heaven, great and doughty beyond telling, Cottus and Briareos and Gyes, presumptuous children. From their shoulders sprang an hundred arms, not to be approached, and each had fifty heads upon his shoulders on their strong limbs, and irresistible was the stubborn strength that was in their great forms.&quot;<br /> <br /> From the text, we can see that the Hecatoncheires are described as being physically large, &quot;\u03bc\u03ad\u03b3\u03b1\u03c2,&quot; in the text, but that same adjective is not used to describe the Gigantes we saw in our first passage. The Elder Cyclopes were not even described as being large, and instead were simply physically described as being like the Gods, but with only one eye.<br /> <br /> Now, the reason this is significant is because the Greeks were <i>obsessed</i> with taxonomy for their world, they ordered and structured things incredibly well, and each of these groups of beings was regarded as distinct from each other. The Elder Cyclopes, the Gigantes, and the Hecatoncheires are all described originally as being distinct, different groups of beings. Even in physical description they are rather different. The Elder Cyclopes are simply described as being in the same visage as the Gods, but with only one eye. The Hecatoncheires are described as being physically large, with fifty heads and one hundred arms sprouting from their shoulders. The Gigantes on the other hand are not described as being big, but armored. Now, in later stories we do see the Gigantes being big, but they gain another physical aberration, legs of snakes.<br /> <br /> So, in total we have a) Three men who look like the Gods but have one eye in the middle of their faces, b) Three large men who are large and have fifty heads and one hundred arms, and c) a species of beings born in armor and wielding spears who are later further described as being large, and having snakes for legs. The original text never says the Elder Cyclopes were One-Eyed Giants, that's just how we started describing them as part of the translation, adding in the 'Giant' terminology. The Hecatoncheires are described as being 'big' as an adjective, but we often translate it as, &quot;Hundred Armed Giants,&quot; even though the term &quot;\u03bc\u03ad\u03b3\u03b1\u03c2,&quot; the adjective of 'big' given to them, is totally different from the noun, &quot;\u0393\u03af\u03b3\u03b1\u03bd\u03c4\u03b1\u03c2,&quot; which is the actual group of Gigantes.<br /> <br /> Even in cosmological function, these three groups are very different. The Elder Cyclopes are only three beings who forge the three most powerful tools of the Three Kings of the Theoi, with their names all relating to Thunder, Storms, and the sort. Likely connecting the sound of their hammering with the sound of storms, their blows on their anvil like thunderclaps. The Hecatoncheires are similarly given names rooted in words of Storms, likely due to them being the beings who control the winds on command of Zeus. They stand at the gates of Tartarus to open and close the doors to let out the storm winds of Typhon trapped within when Zeus needs them. The Gigantes don't have any similarities with these, being a species rather than specific named individuals, and very clearly having a primary motif of warfare which will become important in the later story of their war against the Gods.<br /> <br /> So, while they are described as Giants, they're not all Giants. The Hecatoncheires are Giants (Adjective), the Cyclopes are actually not ever described as being large but I suspect the Younger Cyclopes may be (I would need to check an original version of The Odyssey I expect) and that confused everything. The Gigantes are Giants (Noun) but never technically described in their first appearance as being Giant. (Adjective) Since the Greeks went to the trouble to differentiate all of these beings to a high degree, I suspect that it is simply meant to be that they are all distinctly different, as different as the Gigantes are from the Erinyes, and the Nymphs for example.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-11T06:37:50-07:00","id":1148801,"title":"","content":"Cyclopes being giant in our conception relative to the normally manifest forms associated with the gods is probably thanks to Homer and Polyphemus.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-11T07:59:36-07:00","id":1148819,"title":"","content":"I Think something like this may be cool for some Crazed group of FBI agents to be looking for, be stringed along by some bored god... maybe Loki, then have him come in laugh in their faces and tell them it doesnt exist.<br /> <br /> sorry, but combat with the gods is something humans will do only through proxy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night